Z||000004||tesmith66||12-20-2001||07:53 AM||||You can't do much to the 2.8. You can rebuild it, pop in a cam, port the exhaust manifolds, up the compression, port the heads, etc.<P>You can spend a TON of money on that engine and still never see 200 horsepower.<P>You could use nitrous and/or a supercharger, but by then, you've spent more than a swap would cost.<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||12-20-2001||10:26 AM||||For more HP the least expensive way is the 3.4 swap. For less than $2000 in parts you can be at or just above 200hp + more torque.<BR>Even going to a stock 3.4 you would be about 180HP. You can get 3.4 crate motors (long blocks) from GM for about $1500 or less if you know someone or find one at a junkyard that has low mileage for a lot less. To rebuild the 2.8 to get to near 180HP you would be spending about $2500.  Even if you restroked it to a 3.1 and bore it to a 3.2, 200hp is hard to get above without going to a supercharger or NOS. You could build a 2.8 for max horsepower and get it over 200 normally aspirated but like any engine you start to sacrifice reliability when you start pushing the limits of the block. With a 3.4 and a supercharger you can get to 250HP.<BR>The 3.4 is basically the same block as a 2.8 so all your stuff swaps over. (Intake system, exhaust manifolds, valve covers, brakets, etc) Since all the external stuff swaps over it will still look like the original 2.8L on the outside. It's the easiest swap to do for staying with the smaller V-6. <P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||next level trends prez||12-20-2001||11:43 AM||||i think the 3.4 would be the best way to go..what all do i need from the 3.4 will it all bolt up i dont want to have to change monting brackets and stuff... do i just need the block or what... if i get the motor is there anybody near tampa fl that can swap it for me... how much will a chip do for me what all will it do i found one at the fiero store... the thermo and street chip... which one should i get for the time being.. and i know where i can get a 38oo engines for 600 bucks with only 60,000 on it.. how hard is it to put in||||reg||1
Z||000007||MinnGreenGT||12-20-2001||12:56 PM||||The 3.4 Block is physically the same size as the 2.8 block.  You'll even use your existing heads & intake setup.  You may want to have the Heads Port & Polished, as well as porting the exhaust manifolds.  Darrell Morse (see link at top of page for vendors) has a Ported Throttlebody and intake plenum available. This and things like a K&N filter and underdrive pulleys will help increase power.<P>As for the "chips"- there is usually an ongoing debate about whether or not there really is any performance gain from them. Most poeple say no- save your money.  I'd suggest the underdrive pulley kit from the FieroStore- you'll actually get some performance gain from it (and it'll cost less).<P>When you say "all motor" I assume that means you're not looking to go into nitrous.  A member here was able to get his 2.8 (with some futher mods) down to 12.9 1/4 mile with a nitrous NX kit (ask around about 1FST2M6 or search for nitrous and his name) check out <A HREF="http://www.nitrouswarehouse.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.nitrouswarehouse.com</A>  and read up on it here: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000002/HTML/20010924-1-006347.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000002/HTML/20010924-1-006347.html</A> <P>And on to the 3800... it is a completely different engine, and therefore requires extensive work to install (especially if you're worried about doing the 3.4).  The wiring needs to be completely reworked and you'll need to make (at least some) custom engine mounts.<P>If you really want the power, Archie ( <A HREF="http://www.v8archie" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie</A>  ) can install a V8 into your fiero for somwhere between $5700 and $7500 (+ motor cost) depending on the engine and your cars setup. Read about it here: <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/arch10.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/arch10.htm</A> <P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||12-20-2001||12:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by next level trends prez:<BR><B>i think the 3.4 would be the best way to go..what all do i need from the 3.4 will it all bolt up i dont want to have to change monting brackets and stuff... do i just need the block or what... if i get the motor is there anybody near tampa fl that can swap it for me... how much will a chip do for me what all will it do i found one at the fiero store... the thermo and street chip... which one should i get for the time being.. and i know where i can get a 38oo engines for 600 bucks with only 60,000 on it.. how hard is it to put in</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>1) Need to modify the 3.4 block a little... I.E. drill in a set of starter mounting bosses. No big deal.<BR>2) Chip and thermo will do JACK-ALL for your purposes. Save your money. The power benefit from such "bolt-ons" is negligable. Better off saving the cash on some more radical internal mods, or to a swap.<BR>3) 3800 is a bit more of an involved swap, need some cradle modifications, and a bit of creative wiring... But it's a good swap. <P>If you don't have the cash, you can't play the game... Sorry...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Another thing to consider might be the Caddillac 4.x motor. It's not a high-power v-8, but it IS a stump-puller, and will probably kill anything this side of $100k from light-light racing. The motor almost bolts in, wiring is simplistic, the only bugger is modifying the cradle, and piping the exhaust. The only real drawback is the almost total lack of aftermarket. You can go to  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/016158.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/016158.html</A>  The swap is being discussed there. <BR>||||reg||7
